use std
into pcbx.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with px_ is exported). It writes one printed circuit board as an IPC-2581 revision C XML file and as an ODB++ 8.1 job (a set of text files). Lengths are millimeters, y points up, angles are degrees.

Module state. Growable real arrays: `XD` (the board numbers JavaScript writes), `FI` (4 per ODB++ file: path offset, path length, data offset and data length, all in `OUT`), `SY` (3 per symbol: kind (0 round, 1 rectangle, 2 oval), width, height), `LW` (one line width per line descriptor). Growable byte lists: `XS` (the string table JavaScript writes, strings separated by line feeds), `OUT` (the result), `TB` (a second buffer for text that is written before its header). Growable int arrays: `SA`, `SB` (start and end offset in `XS` of every string), `CN` (per part: its number among the parts on its side), `SUB` (per pad: its number among the earlier pads of its net), `MK` (per part: a mark), `RW` (ODB++ matrix row codes), `FC` (per pad and stack position: copper feature number or -1), `FV` (per via and stack position: copper feature number or -1), `FT` (per track), `FZ` (per poured rectangle), `FM`, `FS` (2 per pad: top and bottom solder mask and paste feature numbers), `FH` (per pad: drill feature), `FHV` (per via: drill feature). Ints, all 0 at start: `NN` (nets, net 0 meaning no net), `NQ` (parts), `NK` (packages), `NF` (footprint pads), `NP` (pads), `NT` (tracks), `NV` (vias), `NZ` (poured rectangles), `NB` (part bodies), `NL` (copper layers), the record offsets `XQ`, `XK`, `XF`, `XP`, `XT`, `XV`, `XZ`, `XB`, and `NFT` (features written so far on the current ODB++ layer). Reals: `BX0`, `BY0`, `BX1`, `BY1` (a package box).

Board numbers in `XD`: [0] to [3] the board left, bottom, right and top; [4] the copper layer count; [5] board thickness; [6] outer copper thickness; [7] inner copper thickness; [8] via pad diameter; [9] via drill; [10] solder mask expansion; [11] silkscreen line width; [12] outline line width; [13] to [21] the counts NN, NQ, NK, NF, NP, NT, NV, NZ, NB; [22] to [29] the plane net of copper layers 0 to 7 (0 when not a plane). Records follow from index 32: parts (8 each: x, y, rotation counterclockwise, side (0 top, 1 bottom), package, first pad, pad count, unused), packages (4 each: first footprint pad, footprint pad count, body width, body height), footprint pads (8 each, in the package's own frame: x, y, width, height, shape (0 rectangle, 1 round or oval), drill (0 for a surface pad), pin number, layer mask), pads (10 each, placed on the board: x, y, width, height, shape, net, layer mask, drill, part, pin number), tracks (7 each: x0, y0, x1, y1, width, layer, net), vias (3 each: x, y, net), poured rectangles (6 each: left, bottom, right, top, net, layer) and part bodies (7 each: left, bottom, right, top, height, side, part). Copper layer 0 is the top, 1 the bottom and 2 to 7 the inner layers. Strings in `XS`: string 0 is the board name, 1 the creation time as "YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss", then the NN net names (net n is string 2 plus n; net 0's is empty), then the NQ part references (part q is string 2 plus NN plus q), the NQ part values (string 2 plus NN plus NQ plus q) and the NK package names (string 2 plus NN plus 2·NQ plus k).

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n)` takes a real growable array by reference: it is emptied, gets `reserve`(n plus 1) and is resized to n zeros. `zi(xs, n, x)` does the same for an int growable array, resizing to n entries equal to x. `zb(xs, n)` empties a byte list, reserves n plus 1 and resizes it to n zero bytes. `rb(xs, n)` empties a byte list and reserves n. On the web a growable array cannot grow past the room it first reserved, so every array is emptied and reserved again before use.

```amni
extern fn px_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "px_view_f64";
extern fn px_view_u8(id i32, xs u8[:]) = "px_view_u8";
```

The exported function (declared with the export keyword) `px_views()` returns an i32 0 after registering f64 view 1 `XD`, f64 view 2 `FI`, u8 view 3 `XS` and u8 view 4 `OUT` with px_view_f64 and px_view_u8. The exported function (declared with the export keyword) `px_setup(nd, ns)` takes two i32 sizes, sizes `XD` with `zf` to nd, `XS` with `zb` to ns, `FI` with `zf` to 1 and `OUT` with `zb` to 1, calls `px_views` and returns 0.

`prepare()` reads the counts and builds the work arrays. `NL` is XD[4] as int clamped to 1 through 8; `NN`, `NQ`, `NK`, `NF`, `NP`, `NT`, `NV`, `NZ` and `NB` are XD[13] to XD[21] as ints. The offsets: `XQ` is 32, `XK` is XQ plus 8·NQ, `XF` is XK plus 4·NK, `XP` is XF plus 8·NF, `XT` is XP plus 10·NP, `XV` is XT plus 7·NT, `XZ` is XV plus 3·NV, `XB` is XZ plus 6·NZ. `SA` and `SB` are emptied and reserved the count of line feeds in XS plus 2; the strings are the pieces of XS between line feeds (the first starts at 0, each next one just after a line feed, each ends at the next line feed or at the end of XS), so k line feeds give k plus 1 strings. `CN` gets with `zi` NQ zeros and then per part q the count of earlier parts with the same `side_of`. `SUB` gets NP zeros and per pad p the count of earlier pads whose net (pd(p, 5) as int) is the same, counted with an int array of NN plus 1 zeros. `MK` gets NQ zeros. `SY` is emptied and reserved 3·(NP plus NF plus NT plus NV plus 16); `LW` is emptied and reserved NT plus NB plus 16. With r equal to 65536 plus 320·(NP·(NL plus 4) plus NV·(NL plus 2) plus 2·NZ plus NT plus 5·NB plus 2·NF plus 2·NQ plus NN) plus 4 times the length of XS, `OUT` and `TB` are emptied and reserved r with `rb`.

Accessors, all taking ints. `str(k)` is the bytes of `XS` from SA[k] to SB[k]. `net_name(n)` is str(2 plus n); `ref_of(q)` is str(2 plus NN plus q); `val_of(q)` is str(2 plus NN plus NQ plus q); `pkg_name(k)` is str(2 plus NN plus 2·NQ plus k). `pt(q, i)` is XD[XQ plus 8q plus i]; `pk(k, i)` is XD[XK plus 4k plus i]; `fp(f, i)` is XD[XF plus 8f plus i]; `pd(p, i)` is XD[XP plus 10p plus i]; `tk(t, i)` is XD[XT plus 7t plus i]; `vi(v, i)` is XD[XV plus 3v plus i]; `zr(z, i)` is XD[XZ plus 6z plus i]; `bd(b, i)` is XD[XB plus 7b plus i]. `slay(s)` is the copper layer at stack position s: 0 when s is 0, 1 when s equals NL minus 1 and NL is above 1, otherwise s plus 1. `spos(l)` is the stack position of copper layer l: 0 when l is 0, NL minus 1 when l is 1, otherwise l minus 1. `onl(p, l)` is true when pd(p, 7) is above 0 or bit l of pd(p, 6) as int is set. `cut(l)` is XD[6] when l is below 2 and XD[7] otherwise. `side_of(q)` is 1 when pt(q, 3) is above 0.5 and 0 otherwise. `rot_of(q)` is pt(q, 2) brought into 0 up to (not including) 360 by adding 360 while it is negative and subtracting 360 while it is at least 360. `pnet(p)` is pd(p, 5) as int and `ppart(p)` is pd(p, 8) as int.

Writers; each takes a byte list `o` by reference and appends to it. `put(o, s)` appends the bytes s. `lf(o)` appends a line feed. `num(o, v)` writes v with std `format_flt` and 4 decimals, then removes trailing zeros after the point and then a trailing point; "-0" becomes "0". `inum(o, n)` writes the int n in decimal. `esc(o, s)` writes the bytes s for XML: & as "&amp;", < as "&lt;", > as "&gt;", the double quote as "&quot;", the single quote as "&apos;", every other byte unchanged. `tok(o, s)` writes s as one ODB++ word: every byte that is a space, a semicolon, a single quote or below 33 becomes '_', every other byte is unchanged, and an empty s writes "_". `qtok(o, s)` writes s with every single quote and every byte below 32 turned into '_'. `xy(o, a, b, x, y)` writes a space, a, "=\"", num(x), "\" ", b, "=\"", num(y) and "\"", a pair of XML attributes. `lname(o, l, lower)` writes the name of copper layer l: "TOP" for 0, "BOTTOM" for 1 and "IN" followed by l minus 1 otherwise, all lowercase when lower is true. `bname(o)` writes `esc` of string 0, or "board" when string 0 is empty. `plyr(o, q)` writes "BOTTOM" when side_of(q) is 1 and NL is above 1, otherwise "TOP". `oem(o, q)` writes esc of the package name of part q (package pt(q, 4) as int), "_" and esc of its value. `pinref(o, p)` writes `<PinRef componentRef="R" pin="n"/>` and a line feed, with R esc of the reference of part ppart(p) and n pd(p, 9) as int. `nset(o, n, extra)` writes "<Set", then " net=\"" esc of the net name and "\"" when n is above 0, then the bytes extra, then ">" and a line feed.

Symbols and line widths. `sym(k, w, h)` (k an int) returns the index of the symbol in `SY` whose kind equals k and whose width and height are within 1e-9 of w and h, appending k as real, w and h when none matches. `shsym(shape, w, h, e)` returns sym(1, w plus 2e, h plus 2e) when shape is below 0.5, sym(0, w plus 2e, w plus 2e) when w and h are within 1e-9 of each other, and sym(2, w plus 2e, h plus 2e) otherwise. `padsym(p, e)` is shsym(pd(p, 4), pd(p, 2), pd(p, 3), e). `lwi(w)` returns the index in `LW` of a width within 1e-9 of w, appending w when none matches. `odbsym(o, i)` writes symbol i of `SY` by its ODB++ name with sizes in micrometers (the millimeter value times 1000 written with `num`): kind 0 "r" and the width; kind 1 "rect", the width, "x" and the height; kind 2 "oval", the width, "x" and the height.

`poly(o, x0, y0, x1, y1)` writes the closed clockwise loop of a rectangle as IPC-2581 points, each element followed by a line feed: `<PolyBegin x="x0" y="y0"/>` (written with `xy`), then `<PolyStepSegment x=".." y=".."/>` for (x0, y1), (x1, y1), (x1, y0) and (x0, y0). `surf(o, x0, y0, x1, y1)` writes the same rectangle as an ODB++ surface, one record per line: "S P 0", "OB x0 y0 I", "OS x0 y1", "OS x1 y1", "OS x1 y0", "OS x0 y0", "OE", "SE", numbers written with `num` and separated by single spaces.

`pkbox(k)` sets `BX0`, `BY0`, `BX1`, `BY1` to the box of package k: when pk(k, 2) is above 0 the body rectangle centered on the origin (from minus half of pk(k, 2) to plus half in x, and the same with pk(k, 3) in y), otherwise 1e30, 1e30, -1e30, -1e30; then it grows the box to contain every footprint pad f of k (pk(k, 1) as int pads starting at pk(k, 0) as int) as the rectangle from fp(f, 0) minus fp(f, 2)/2, fp(f, 1) minus fp(f, 3)/2 to fp(f, 0) plus fp(f, 2)/2, fp(f, 1) plus fp(f, 3)/2. When BX0 is still above BX1 the box becomes -0.5, -0.5, 0.5, 0.5. `pitch(k)` is the smallest distance between the centers of two different footprint pads of k, or 0 when it has fewer than 2. The file already imports math at its top, so this block starts straight with its functions and adds no import line. Helium has no int() call: every real-to-int conversion here is a cast written with as int.

`ipc_layers(o, mode)` (mode an int) writes the layer list in stack order. For each layer with name N, function F and side S, mode 0 writes `<LayerRef name="N"/>` and mode 1 writes `<Layer name="N" layerFunction="F" side="S" polarity="POSITIVE"/>`, each followed by a line feed. In order: "SILK_TOP" SILKSCREEN TOP; "PASTE_TOP" SOLDERPASTE TOP; "MASK_TOP" SOLDERMASK TOP; then for every stack position s from 0 to NL minus 1 the copper layer slay(s) named by `lname` in upper case with function PLANE when XD[22 plus slay(s)] is above 0.5 and CONDUCTOR otherwise and side TOP when s is 0, BOTTOM when s equals NL minus 1 and NL is above 1, INTERNAL otherwise, and when s is below NL minus 1 after it the dielectric "DIEL_" followed by s plus 1 with function DIELCORE and side INTERNAL; when NL is above 1 "MASK_BOTTOM" SOLDERMASK BOTTOM and "PASTE_BOTTOM" SOLDERPASTE BOTTOM; "SILK_BOTTOM" SILKSCREEN BOTTOM; "OUTLINE" BOARD_OUTLINE ALL; last "DRILL" DRILL ALL, which mode 1 writes as `<Layer name="DRILL" layerFunction="DRILL" side="ALL" polarity="POSITIVE">`, a line feed, `<Span fromLayer="TOP" toLayer="T"/>`, a line feed, `</Layer>` and a line feed, with T "BOTTOM" when NL is above 1 and "TOP" otherwise.

`ipc_head(o)` writes, each followed by a line feed: `<LogisticHeader>`, `<Role id="Owner" roleFunction="SENDER"/>`, `<Enterprise id="Amni-Scient" code="NONE"/>`, `<Person name="Amni-CAD" enterpriseRef="Amni-Scient" roleRef="Owner"/>`, `</LogisticHeader>`, `<HistoryRecord number="1" origination="D" software="Amni-CAD" lastChange="D">` (D esc of string 1), `<FileRevision fileRevisionId="1" comment="NONE" label="NONE">`, `<SoftwarePackage name="Amni-CAD" revision="1" vendor="Amni-Scient">`, `<Certification certificationStatus="SELFTEST"/>`, `</SoftwarePackage>`, `</FileRevision>`, `</HistoryRecord>`.

`ipc_bom(o)` writes `<Bom name="N_bom">`, `<BomHeader assembly="N" revision="1">`, `<StepRef name="N"/>` and `</BomHeader>` (N written by `bname`). Then, with every `MK` entry set to 0, for every part q in order whose mark is 0, its group is q and every later part r with pt(r, 4) as int equal to pt(q, 4) as int and a value equal byte for byte to q's; every member is marked 1. For the group it writes `<BomItem OEMDesignNumberRef="O" quantity="G" pinCount="C" category="ELECTRICAL">` (O by `oem`(o, q), G the group size, C pt(q, 6) as int), then for every member r `<RefDes name="R" packageRef="P" populate="true" layerRef="L"/>` (R esc of its reference, P esc of its package name, L by `plyr`), then `<Characteristics category="ELECTRICAL">`, `<Textual definitionSource="Amni-CAD" textualCharacteristicName="Value" textualCharacteristicValue="V"/>` (V esc of the value of q), `</Characteristics>` and `</BomItem>`. Last it writes `</Bom>`. Each tag is followed by a line feed.

`ipc_stack(o)` writes `<Stackup name="STACKUP" overallThickness="T" tolPlus="0" tolMinus="0" whereMeasured="METAL">` and `<StackupGroup name="GROUP" thickness="T" tolPlus="0" tolMinus="0">` with T XD[5] written by `num`. With d the larger of 0.01 and (XD[5] minus the sum of cut(slay(s)) over every stack position s) divided by the larger of 1 and NL minus 1, and a sequence number q starting at 1 and going up by 1 per layer written: for every stack position s, `<StackupLayer layerOrGroupRef="N" thickness="c" tolPlus="0" tolMinus="0" sequence="q"/>` for its copper layer (N the upper-case `lname` of slay(s), c cut(slay(s))), then when s is below NL minus 1 the same for "DIEL_" followed by s plus 1 with thickness d. Then `</StackupGroup>` and `</Stackup>`. Each tag is followed by a line feed.

`ipc_packages(o)` writes for every package k: `<Package name="P" type="OTHER" pinOne="n">` (P esc of the package name, n the pin number of its first footprint pad as int), `<Outline>`, `<Polygon>`, then `poly` of the box from `pkbox`(k), `</Polygon>`, `<LineDescRef id="LINE_i"/>` with i `lwi`(XD[11]), `</Outline>`, then for each of its footprint pads f: `<Pin number="n" type="T" electricalType="ELECTRICAL">` (n fp(f, 6) as int, T "THRU" when fp(f, 5) is above 0 and "SURFACE" otherwise), `<Location x="x" y="y"/>` with fp(f, 0) and fp(f, 1), `<StandardPrimitiveRef id="PRIM_i"/>` with i shsym(fp(f, 4), fp(f, 2), fp(f, 3), 0) and `</Pin>`; last `</Package>`. Each tag is followed by a line feed.

`ipc_components(o)` writes for every part q: `<Component refDes="R" packageRef="P" part="O" layerRef="L" mountType="M">` (R esc of the reference, P esc of the package name of pt(q, 4) as int, O by `oem`, L by `plyr`, M "THMT" when any of its pads (pt(q, 6) as int pads starting at pt(q, 5) as int) has pd(p, 7) above 0 and "SMT" otherwise); then when rot_of(q) is above 1e-9 or side_of(q) is 1, `<Xform rotation="r"/>` with r rot_of(q) by `num` and with ` mirror="true"` before the "/>" when side_of(q) is 1; then `<Location x="x" y="y"/>` with pt(q, 0) and pt(q, 1), and `</Component>`. Each tag is followed by a line feed.

`ipc_nets(o)` writes for every net n from 1 to NN minus 1 that some pad has (pnet(p) equal to n): `<LogicalNet name="N">` and a line feed (N esc of the net name), then `pinref`(o, p) for every pad p of net n, then `</LogicalNet>` and a line feed.

`ipc_copper(o, s)` writes the copper of stack position s, with l equal to slay(s). When NV is 0, no track or poured rectangle has layer l (as int) and no pad is on l (`onl`), it writes nothing. Otherwise it writes `<LayerFeature layerRef="N">` (N the upper-case `lname` of l) and then for every net n from 0 to NN minus 1 these groups, each only when it has at least one member: the tracks with layer l and net n (tk(t, 5) and tk(t, 6) as ints), written as `nset`(o, n, "") then `<Features>`, `<UserSpecial>`, for each track `<Line startX="x0" startY="y0" endX="x1" endY="y1">`, `<LineDescRef id="LINE_i"/>` with i lwi(tk(t, 4)) and `</Line>`, then `</UserSpecial>`, `</Features>`, `</Set>`; the vias of net n (vi(v, 2) as int) as nset(o, n, " padUsage=\"VIA\"") then for each via `<Pad>`, `<Location x="x" y="y"/>`, `<StandardPrimitiveRef id="PRIM_i"/>` with i sym(0, XD[8], XD[8]) and `</Pad>`, then `</Set>`; the pads on l with net n as nset(o, n, "") then for each pad `<Pad>`, `<Location x="x" y="y"/>`, `<StandardPrimitiveRef id="PRIM_i"/>` with i padsym(p, 0), `pinref`(o, p) and `</Pad>`, then `</Set>`; the poured rectangles with layer l and net n (zr(z, 5) and zr(z, 4) as ints) as nset(o, n, "") then `<Features>`, `<UserSpecial>`, for each rectangle `<Contour>`, `<Polygon>`, `poly` of it, `</Polygon>` and `</Contour>`, then `</UserSpecial>`, `</Features>`, `</Set>`. Last `</LayerFeature>`. Each tag is followed by a line feed.

`ipc_others(o)` writes the other layers, each as `<LayerFeature layerRef="N">`, its sets and `</LayerFeature>`, and only when it has at least one item; each tag is followed by a line feed. Solder mask: "MASK_TOP" with b 1 and, when NL is above 1, "MASK_BOTTOM" with b 2: `<Set>` and a line feed, then for every pad p whose mask (pd(p, 6) as int) bitwise and b is not 0: `<Pad>`, `<Location x="x" y="y"/>`, `<StandardPrimitiveRef id="PRIM_i"/>` with i padsym(p, XD[10]), `pinref`(o, p) and `</Pad>`; then `</Set>`. Paste: "PASTE_TOP" with b 1 and, when NL is above 1, "PASTE_BOTTOM" with b 2: the same for the pads with that bit and pd(p, 7) equal to 0, with i padsym(p, 0). Silkscreen: "SILK_TOP" with side 0 and "SILK_BOTTOM" with side 1: `<Set>`, `<Features>`, `<UserSpecial>`, then for every body whose bd(b, 5) as int equals the side `<Polyline>`, `poly` of its rectangle, `<LineDescRef id="LINE_i"/>` with i lwi(XD[11]) and `</Polyline>`; then `</UserSpecial>`, `</Features>`, `</Set>`. "OUTLINE", always: `<Set>`, `<Features>`, `<UserSpecial>`, `<Polyline>`, `poly` of XD[0], XD[1], XD[2], XD[3], `<LineDescRef id="LINE_i"/>` with i lwi(XD[12]), `</Polyline>`, `</UserSpecial>`, `</Features>`, `</Set>`. "DRILL": with a hole number h starting at 1, for every net n from 0 to NN minus 1 that has a pad with pd(p, 7) above 0 or a via: `nset`(o, n, ""), then for each such pad `<Hole name="Hh" diameter="d" platingStatus="PLATED" plusTol="0" minusTol="0" x="x" y="y"/>` (d pd(p, 7)) and for each via of net n the same with diameter XD[9] and platingStatus "VIA", h going up by 1 per hole; then `</Set>`.

The exported function (declared with the export keyword) `px_ipc()` returns an i32, the length of `OUT`. It calls `prepare()`. Into `TB` it writes in order: `ipc_head`, `ipc_bom`, `<Ecad name="N">` (N by `bname`), `<CadHeader units="MILLIMETER"/>`, `<CadData>`, `ipc_layers`(TB, 1), `ipc_stack`, `<Step name="N" type="BOARD">`, `<Datum x="0" y="0"/>`, `<Profile>`, `<Polygon>`, `poly` of XD[0], XD[1], XD[2], XD[3], `</Polygon>`, `</Profile>`, `ipc_packages`, `ipc_components`, `ipc_nets`, `ipc_copper`(TB, s) for every stack position s from 0 to NL minus 1, `ipc_others`, then `</Step>`, `</CadData>` and `</Ecad>`. Then `OUT` is emptied keeping its reserve and gets `<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>`, `<IPC-2581 revision="C" xmlns="http://webstds.ipc.org/2581" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">`, `<Content roleRef="Owner">`, `<FunctionMode mode="ASSEMBLY"/>`, `<StepRef name="N"/>`, `ipc_layers`(OUT, 0), `<BomRef name="N_bom"/>`, `<DictionaryLineDesc units="MILLIMETER">`, for every width index i of `LW` `<EntryLineDesc id="LINE_i">`, `<LineDesc lineEnd="ROUND" lineWidth="w"/>` and `</EntryLineDesc>`, then `</DictionaryLineDesc>`, `<DictionaryStandard units="MILLIMETER">`, for every symbol i of `SY` `<EntryStandard id="PRIM_i">`, then `<Circle diameter="w"/>` for kind 0, `<RectCenter width="w" height="h"/>` for kind 1 or `<Oval width="w" height="h"/>` for kind 2, then `</EntryStandard>`; then `</DictionaryStandard>`, `</Content>`, all of `TB`, and `</IPC-2581>`. Every tag named in this paragraph is followed by a line feed. It calls `px_views` and returns the length of OUT as i32.

ODB++ job helpers. `job(o)` writes the job name: string 0 with ASCII capitals lowercased and every byte that is then not a lowercase letter, a digit, '_' or '-' turned into '_'; "board" when string 0 is empty. `fstart()` appends the length of `OUT` to `FI`, then writes `job`(OUT) and "/" into OUT; the caller then writes the rest of the path. `fdata()` appends to FI the length of OUT minus the last path offset (the path length) and then the length of OUT (the data offset). `fend()` appends the length of OUT minus the last data offset (the data length). `odb_date(o)` writes string 1 as "YYYYMMDD.hhmmss": its bytes 0 to 3, 5 and 6, 8 and 9, a point, then bytes 11 and 12, 14 and 15, 17 and 18; when string 1 is shorter than 19 bytes it writes "20260101.000000".

`rows()` empties `RW`, reserves NL plus 16 and appends the matrix row codes in order: 1, 2, 3, 4, then 10 plus slay(s) for every stack position s, then 5 and 6 when NL is above 1, then 7, 8 and 9. `rowname(o, c)` writes the name of row code c: 1 "comp_+_top", 2 "sst", 3 "spt", 4 "smt", 5 "smb", 6 "spb", 7 "ssb", 8 "comp_+_bot", 9 "drill", and for c of at least 10 `lname`(o, c minus 10, true). `rowtype(o, c)` writes "COMPONENT" for 1 and 8, "SILK_SCREEN" for 2 and 7, "SOLDER_PASTE" for 3 and 6, "SOLDER_MASK" for 4 and 5, "DRILL" for 9, and for c of at least 10 "POWER_GROUND" when XD[22 plus c minus 10] is above 0.5 and "SIGNAL" otherwise. `lyr(c)` is the index of row code c in the EDA layer list (copper in stack order, then smt, spt, smb, spb, drill): spos(c minus 10) for c of at least 10, NL for 4, NL plus 1 for 3, NL plus 2 for 5, NL plus 3 for 6, and for 9 NL plus 4 when NL is above 1 and NL plus 2 otherwise.

`odb_matrix(o)` writes the lines "STEP {", "   COL=1", "   NAME=pcb", "}" and an empty line, then for every row code c at position i of `RW`: "LAYER {", "   ROW=" with i plus 1, "   CONTEXT=BOARD", "   TYPE=" with `rowtype`, "   NAME=" with `rowname`, "   POLARITY=POSITIVE", "   START_NAME=" and "   END_NAME=" (for c 9 followed by "top" and by "bottom", or by "top" and "top" when NL is 1; for other rows nothing follows), "   OLD_NAME=", "}" and an empty line. Each line ends in a line feed.

`odb_info(o)` writes the lines "JOB_NAME=" with `job`, "UNITS=MM", "ODB_VERSION_MAJOR=8", "ODB_VERSION_MINOR=1", "ODB_SOURCE=Amni-CAD", "CREATION_DATE=" with `odb_date`, "SAVE_DATE=" with `odb_date`, "SAVE_APP=Amni-CAD", "SAVE_USER=amni-cad" and "MAX_UID=0". `odb_stephdr(o)` writes the lines "UNITS=MM", "X_DATUM=0", "Y_DATUM=0", "X_ORIGIN=0", "Y_ORIGIN=0", "TOP_ACTIVE=0", "BOTTOM_ACTIVE=0", "RIGHT_ACTIVE=0", "LEFT_ACTIVE=0", "AFFECTING_BOM=" and "AFFECTING_BOM_CHANGED=0". `odb_font(o)` writes the lines "XSIZE 0.302000", "YSIZE 0.302000" and "OFFSET 0.000000". `odb_profile(o)` writes the lines "UNITS=MM", "#", "#Num Features", "#", "F 1", "#", "#Layer features", "#", then `surf` of XD[0], XD[1], XD[2], XD[3]. Each line ends in a line feed.

`odb_feats(c)` writes the features file of row code c into `OUT`. It empties `TB` and `SY` keeping their reserves and sets `NFT` to 0, then writes the feature records into TB, each line ending in a line feed, numbers written with `num` and separated by single spaces, and each feature taking the number NFT and then adding 1 to it (a surface is one feature). For c of at least 10, with copper layer l equal to c minus 10 and s equal to spos(l): every track t with tk(t, 5) as int equal to l as "L x0 y0 x1 y1 i P 0" with i sym(0, w, w) (w = tk(t, 4)), FT[t] taking its number; every pad p on l (`onl`) as "P x y i P 0 0;0=0" with i padsym(p, 0), followed by ",1" when pd(p, 7) is 0, FC[p·NL plus s] taking its number; every via v as "P x y i P 0 0;0=1" with i sym(0, XD[8], XD[8]), FV[v·NL plus s] taking its number; every poured rectangle z with zr(z, 5) as int equal to l as `surf` of it, FZ[z] taking its number. For c 4 (b 1) and 5 (b 2): every pad p whose mask pd(p, 6) as int bitwise and b is not 0 as "P x y i P 0 0" with i padsym(p, XD[10]), FM[2p plus b minus 1] taking its number. For c 3 (b 1) and 6 (b 2): every pad with that mask bit and pd(p, 7) equal to 0 as "P x y i P 0 0" with i padsym(p, 0), FS[2p plus b minus 1] taking its number. For c 2 (side 0) and 7 (side 1): for every body b with bd(b, 5) as int equal to the side, four lines "L xa ya xb yb i P 0" with i sym(0, XD[11], XD[11]): left bottom to left top, left top to right top, right top to right bottom and right bottom to left bottom. For c 9: every pad p with pd(p, 7) above 0 as "P x y i P 0 0;2=0" with i sym(0, pd(p, 7), pd(p, 7)), FH[p] taking its number, then every via v as "P x y i P 0 0;2=2" with i sym(0, XD[9], XD[9]), FHV[v] taking its number. Rows 1 and 8 have no features. Then it writes into OUT the lines "UNITS=MM", "#", "#Num Features", "#", "F " with NFT, "#", "#Feature symbol names", "#", for every symbol i "$" with i, a space and `odbsym`(OUT, i), then "#", "#Feature attribute names", "#", "@0 .pad_usage", "@1 .smd", "@2 .drill", "#", "#Layer features", "#", and then all of TB. Helium has no int() call: every real-to-int conversion here is a cast written with as int.

`odb_comps(o, side)` (side an int) writes the lines "UNITS=MM" and "#", then for every part q with side_of(q) equal to side: "# CMP " with CN[q]; "CMP k x y r m R P" with k pt(q, 4) as int, x and y pt(q, 0) and pt(q, 1), r the clockwise rotation (360 minus rot_of(q), brought into 0 up to 360, by `num`), m "M" when side is 1 and "N" otherwise, R `tok` of the reference and P `tok` of the package name followed, when the value is not empty, by "_" and `tok` of the value; "PRP VALUE '" with `qtok` of the value and "'"; then for every pad i from 0 to pt(q, 6) as int minus 1, with p equal to pt(q, 5) as int plus i, "TOP i x y r m n u t" with x and y pd(p, 0) and pd(p, 1), r and m as for the part, n pnet(p), u SUB[p] and t pd(p, 9) as int; and last "#". Each line ends in a line feed. Helium has no int() call: every real-to-int conversion here is a cast written with as int.

`odb_eda(o)` writes the lines "HDR Amni-CAD", "UNITS=MM", "LYR" followed by the EDA layer names in `lyr` order, each after a single space (`lname`(o, slay(s), true) for every stack position s, then "smt", "spt", then "smb" and "spb" when NL is above 1, then "drill"), and "#". Then for every net n from 0 to NN minus 1: "#NET " with n; "NET " with `tok` of the net name, or "NET $NONE$" for net 0; then for every pad p with pnet(p) equal to n, with q its part ppart(p): "SNT TOP S c i" (S "B" when side_of(q) is 1 and "T" otherwise, c CN[q], i p minus pt(q, 5) as int), then "FID C s f" for every stack position s whose f = FC[p·NL plus s] is at least 0, "FID C L f" for each of FM[2p] (L lyr(4)), FM[2p plus 1] (L lyr(5)), FS[2p] (L lyr(3)) and FS[2p plus 1] (L lyr(6)) that is at least 0, and "FID H L f" with L lyr(9) and f FH[p] when it is at least 0; then for every track t of net n (tk(t, 6) as int) "SNT TRC" and "FID C s f" with s spos(tk(t, 5) as int) and f FT[t]; then for every via v of net n "SNT VIA", "FID C s f" for every stack position s with f FV[v·NL plus s] at least 0, and "FID H L f" with L lyr(9) and f FHV[v]; then when some poured rectangle has zr(z, 4) as int equal to n, "SNT PLN S E 0" and "FID C s f" for every such rectangle z with s spos(zr(z, 5) as int) and f FZ[z]. Then for every package k: "# PKG " with k; "PKG P h x0 y0 x1 y1;" with P `tok` of the package name, h `pitch`(k) and the box from `pkbox`(k); "RC x0 y0 w h" with the box's lower left corner, width and height; then for every footprint pad f of k: "PIN n T x y d E M" with n fp(f, 6) as int, T "T" when fp(f, 5) is above 0 and "S" otherwise, x and y fp(f, 0) and fp(f, 1), d fp(f, 5), and M equal to T; then its outline: "CR x y r" with r half of fp(f, 2) when fp(f, 4) is at least 0.5 and fp(f, 2) and fp(f, 3) are within 1e-9 of each other, otherwise "RC" with the lower left corner fp(f, 0) minus fp(f, 2)/2, fp(f, 1) minus fp(f, 3)/2 and the width and height; and last "#". Each line ends in a line feed; numbers are written with `num`. Helium has no int() call: every real-to-int conversion here is a cast written with as int.

The exported function (declared with the export keyword) `px_odb()` returns an i32, the number of files. It calls `prepare()` and `rows()`; empties `FI` and reserves 4 times (the length of RW plus 12); sizes with `zi` `FC` to NP·NL entries -1, `FV` to NV·NL entries -1, `FT` to NT, `FZ` to NZ, `FM` and `FS` to 2·NP, `FH` to NP and `FHV` to NV entries -1; and empties `OUT` keeping its reserve. Then it writes these files, each started with `fstart`, its path after the job folder written into OUT, `fdata`, its content written into OUT and closed with `fend`: "matrix/matrix" with `odb_matrix`, "misc/info" with `odb_info`, "fonts/standard" with `odb_font`, "steps/pcb/stephdr" with `odb_stephdr`, "steps/pcb/profile" with `odb_profile`; then for every row code c of `RW` the path "steps/pcb/layers/", `rowname`(OUT, c) and "/features" with `odb_feats`(c), and for c 1 and 8 also "steps/pcb/layers/", rowname and "/components" with `odb_comps`(OUT, 0 for c 1 and 1 for c 8); last "steps/pcb/eda/data" with `odb_eda`. It calls `px_views` and returns the length of FI divided by 4 as i32.

STEP export state, ints starting at 0: `EN` (the number of the last STEP entity written) and `PDS` (the last product definition shape written). Growable int arrays: `LP` (oriented edges of the loop being built), `BL` (bounds of the face being built), `FL` (faces of the solid being built), `SL` (styled items), `IA` (instance placements), `IR` (instance representations), `IP` (instance product definitions), `IQ` (instance part, -1 for the bare board), `KR` and `KP` (per package its body representation and product definition, 0 when not written yet). Growable real arrays `HX`, `HY`, `HR`: the drill holes cut through the board (center x, center y, radius).

STEP writers. `ent(o)` adds 1 to `EN`, writes "#", EN by `inum` and "=" to o, and returns EN. `eref(o, n)` writes "#" and n by `inum`. `fin(o)` writes ";" and a line feed. Below, "the entity T" means: n is `ent`(o), then the text T is written (every #name in T is that number written with `eref`, every other name a real written with `rnum`), then `fin`(o); n is that entity's number. `rnum(o, v)` writes v by `num` and then a "." when none of the bytes num just added is a "." (so whole numbers come out as 3. and 0.). `sesc(o, s)` writes the bytes of s for a quoted STEP string: an apostrophe is written as two apostrophes, a backslash as two backslashes, and any byte below 32 or above 126 as an underscore. `trip(o, x, y, z)` writes "(", rnum x, ",", rnum y, ",", rnum z and ")". `refs(o, xs)` writes the numbers of the int growable array xs as #n by `eref`, separated by commas.

STEP geometry. `spt(o, x, y, z)` returns the entity CARTESIAN_POINT('', followed by `trip` of x, y, z and ")". `sdir(o, x, y, z)` returns the same with DIRECTION(''. `sax(o, x, y, z, ax, ay, az, rx, ry, rz)` first writes p as spt(o, x, y, z), a as sdir(o, ax, ay, az) and r as sdir(o, rx, ry, rz), then returns the entity AXIS2_PLACEMENT_3D('',#p,#a,#r). `svx(o, x, y, z)` writes p as spt(o, x, y, z) and returns the entity VERTEX_POINT('',#p). `splane(o, x, y, z, ax, ay, az, rx, ry, rz)` writes p as sax of all its arguments and returns the entity PLANE('',#p).

STEP edges. `sline(o, a, b, x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1)` writes the straight edge from vertex a to vertex b: p is spt(o, x0, y0, z0); L is the distance between the two points; d is sdir of (x1 minus x0, y1 minus y0, z1 minus z0) each divided by L; then v is the entity VECTOR('',#d,L), l is the entity LINE('',#p,#v), and it returns the entity EDGE_CURVE('',#a,#b,#l,.T.). `scirc(o, v, cx, cy, z, r)` writes the full circle edge that starts and ends at vertex v: c is sax(o, cx, cy, z, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0), k is the entity CIRCLE('',#c,r), and it returns the entity EDGE_CURVE('',#v,#v,#k,.T.). `soe(o, e, fwd)` writes the entity ORIENTED_EDGE('',*,*,#e,.T.), with .F. in place of .T. when fwd is false, appends it to `LP` and returns it.

STEP faces. `sbound(o, outer)` writes l as the entity EDGE_LOOP('',( then `refs` of `LP` then "))", empties `LP`, then writes the entity FACE_OUTER_BOUND('',#l,.T.) when outer is true or FACE_BOUND('',#l,.T.) when it is false, appends that bound to `BL` and returns it. `sface(o, s, same)` writes the entity ADVANCED_FACE('',( then `refs` of `BL` then "),#s,.T.)" (with .F. in place of .T. when same is false), empties `BL`, appends the face to `FL` and returns it.

`prism(o, x0, y0, x1, y1, z0, z1, holes)` writes a closed box solid from z0 up to z1 over the rectangle x0, y0 to x1, y1, with every hole of `HX`, `HY`, `HR` drilled straight through when holes is true, and returns its MANIFOLD_SOLID_BREP entity. Corners 0 to 3 are (x0, y0), (x1, y0), (x1, y1), (x0, y1); for corner i, j is the next corner (i plus 1, and 0 after 3). Vertices from `svx`: b[i] at corner i and height z0, t[i] at corner i and height z1. Edges from `sline`: eb[i] from b[i] to b[j], et[i] from t[i] to t[j], ev[i] from b[i] to t[i]. When holes is true, for every hole h (x, y, r): vertices vb[h] at (x plus r, y, z0) and vt[h] at (x plus r, y, z1), edges cb[h] as scirc(o, vb[h], x, y, z0, r) and ct[h] as scirc(o, vt[h], x, y, z1, r), and the seam es[h] as sline from vb[h] to vt[h]. These numbers live in local int growable arrays. Then the faces, each a series of `soe` calls closed by `sbound`, more loops when needed, and one `sface`: the top face has the outer loop et[0], et[1], et[2], et[3] all forward, then per hole an inner loop of ct[h] backward, on splane(o, x0, y0, z1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0) with same true; the bottom face has the outer loop eb[3], eb[2], eb[1], eb[0] all backward, then per hole an inner loop of cb[h] forward, on splane(o, x0, y0, z0, 0, 0, -1, 1, 0, 0) with same true; side i (dx, dy the unit direction from corner i to corner j) has the outer loop eb[i] forward, ev[j] forward, et[i] backward, ev[i] backward, on splane(o, corner i x, corner i y, z0, dy, minus dx, 0, dx, dy, 0) with same true; every hole h has the outer loop cb[h] backward, es[h] forward, ct[h] forward, es[h] backward, on the surface c, the entity CYLINDRICAL_SURFACE('',#a,r) with a as sax(o, x, y, z0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0), with same false. Last, s is the entity CLOSED_SHELL('',( then `refs` of `FL` then "))", `FL` is emptied, and it returns the entity MANIFOLD_SOLID_BREP('',#s).

`holes()` empties `HX`, `HY` and `HR`, then offers each drill in turn: every pad p with pd(p, 7) above 0 (center pd(p, 0), pd(p, 1) and radius half of pd(p, 7)), then every via v (center vi(v, 0), vi(v, 1) and radius half of XD[9]). An offered hole (x, y, r) is appended to the three arrays only when r is above 0.01, it lies inside the board with 0.05 to spare (x minus r above XD[0] plus 0.05, x plus r below XD[2] minus 0.05, y minus r above XD[1] plus 0.05, y plus r below XD[3] minus 0.05), and the distance from its center to the center of every hole kept so far is above r plus that hole's radius plus 0.05.

`paint(o, solid, r, g, b)` writes c as the entity COLOUR_RGB('',r,g,b), f as FILL_AREA_STYLE_COLOUR('',#c), a as FILL_AREA_STYLE('',(#f)), s as SURFACE_STYLE_FILL_AREA(#a), d as SURFACE_SIDE_STYLE('',(#s)), u as SURFACE_STYLE_USAGE(.BOTH.,#d), q as PRESENTATION_STYLE_ASSIGNMENT((#u)) and y as STYLED_ITEM('color',(#q),#solid), and appends y to `SL`.

`sname(o)` writes the board name for a STEP string: `sesc` of str(0), or "board" when str(0) is empty. `prod(o, kind, k)` writes a STEP product and returns its product definition; its name is written by `sname` for kind 0, as "PCB" for kind 1 and by `sesc` of pkg_name(k) for kind 2. It writes c as the entity PRODUCT_CONTEXT('',#1,'mechanical'), p as PRODUCT('N','N','',(#c)) with the name in place of each N, f as PRODUCT_DEFINITION_FORMATION('','',#p), x as PRODUCT_DEFINITION_CONTEXT('part definition',#1,'design'), d as PRODUCT_DEFINITION('design','',#f,#x), then the entity PRODUCT_RELATED_PRODUCT_CATEGORY('part',$,(#p)), stores the entity PRODUCT_DEFINITION_SHAPE('','',#d) in `PDS`, and returns d. `solid_part(o, kind, k, solid)` writes a as sax(o, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0), then r as the entity ADVANCED_BREP_SHAPE_REPRESENTATION('N',(#a,#solid),#7) with the name of `prod` for the same kind and k in place of N, then the entity SHAPE_DEFINITION_REPRESENTATION(#PDS,#r), and returns r.

`step_head(o)` writes these lines: "ISO-10303-21;", "HEADER;", "FILE_DESCRIPTION(('Amni-CAD printed circuit board'),'2;1');", then FILE_NAME(' followed by `sname`, ".step','", str(1) and "',(''),(''),'Amni-CAD','Amni-CAD','');" as one line, then "FILE_SCHEMA(('AUTOMOTIVE_DESIGN { 1 0 10303 214 1 1 1 1 }'));", "ENDSEC;", "DATA;" and these seven fixed lines exactly:
"#1=APPLICATION_CONTEXT('core data for automotive mechanical design processes');"
"#2=APPLICATION_PROTOCOL_DEFINITION('international standard','automotive_design',2000,#1);"
"#3=(LENGTH_UNIT()NAMED_UNIT(*)SI_UNIT(.MILLI.,.METRE.));"
"#4=(NAMED_UNIT(*)PLANE_ANGLE_UNIT()SI_UNIT($,.RADIAN.));"
"#5=(NAMED_UNIT(*)SI_UNIT($,.STERADIAN.)SOLID_ANGLE_UNIT());"
"#6=UNCERTAINTY_MEASURE_WITH_UNIT(LENGTH_MEASURE(1.E-07),#3,'distance_accuracy_value','confusion accuracy');"
"#7=(GEOMETRIC_REPRESENTATION_CONTEXT(3)GLOBAL_UNCERTAINTY_ASSIGNED_CONTEXT((#6))GLOBAL_UNIT_ASSIGNED_CONTEXT((#3,#4,#5))REPRESENTATION_CONTEXT('Context #1','3D Context with UNIT and UNCERTAINTY'));"
and sets `EN` to 7.

`step_parts(o)` writes the board and every part body. The board: d is prod(o, 1, 0), s is prism(o, XD[0], XD[1], XD[2], XD[3], 0, XD[5], true), r is solid_part(o, 1, 0, s), then paint(o, s, 0.08, 0.37, 0.18); it appends r to `IR`, d to `IP`, -1 to `IQ` and sax(o, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0) to `IA`. Then every part q from 0 below NQ, with k its package (pt(q, 4) cast with as int) and e its body height pt(q, 7), when pk(k, 2), pk(k, 3) and e are all above 0: if `KR`[k] is 0 the package body is written first: cx and cy are the middles between the smallest and largest x and y of its footprint pads (fp(f, 0) and fp(f, 1) for the pk(k, 1) pads starting at pk(k, 0), both cast with as int; 0 and 0 when it has none), d is prod(o, 2, k), s is prism(o, cx minus half pk(k, 2), cy minus half pk(k, 3), cx plus half pk(k, 2), cy plus half pk(k, 3), 0, e, false), `KR`[k] is solid_part(o, 2, k, s), `KP`[k] is d, and paint(o, s, 0.16, 0.16, 0.17). Then with c and n the cosine and sine of pt(q, 2) degrees (each set to exactly 0 when within 1e-9 of 0), it appends KR[k] to `IR`, KP[k] to `IP`, q to `IQ` and to `IA` the placement sax(o, pt(q, 0), pt(q, 1), XD[5], 0, 0, 1, c, n, 0) for a top part (pt(q, 3) is 0) or sax(o, pt(q, 0), pt(q, 1), 0, 0, 0, -1, minus c, minus n, 0) for a bottom part.

`step_asm(o)` writes the assembly that places them: d is prod(o, 0, 0), a is sax(o, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0), t is the entity SHAPE_REPRESENTATION(' followed by `sname`, "',(#a," then `refs` of `IA` and "),#7)"; then the entity SHAPE_DEFINITION_REPRESENTATION(#PDS,#t). Then for every instance i: its name N is "PCB" when IQ[i] is -1 and otherwise `sesc` of ref_of(IQ[i]); n is the entity NEXT_ASSEMBLY_USAGE_OCCURRENCE('N','N','',#d,#IP[i],$), p is PRODUCT_DEFINITION_SHAPE('Placement','Placement of an item',#n), x is ITEM_DEFINED_TRANSFORMATION('','',#a,#IA[i]), r is (REPRESENTATION_RELATIONSHIP('','',#IR[i],#t)REPRESENTATION_RELATIONSHIP_WITH_TRANSFORMATION(#x)SHAPE_REPRESENTATION_RELATIONSHIP()), then the entity CONTEXT_DEPENDENT_SHAPE_REPRESENTATION(#r,#p). Last the entity MECHANICAL_DESIGN_GEOMETRIC_PRESENTATION_REPRESENTATION('',( then `refs` of `SL` then "),#7)".

The exported function (declared with the export keyword) `px_step()` returns an i32, the length of `OUT`. It calls `prepare()` and `holes()`; empties `OUT`, `LP`, `BL`, `FL`, `SL`, `IA`, `IR`, `IP` and `IQ`; sizes `KR` and `KP` to NK zeros with `zi`; then calls `step_head`(OUT), `step_parts`(OUT) and `step_asm`(OUT), writes the lines "ENDSEC;" and "END-ISO-10303-21;", calls `px_views` and returns the length of OUT as i32. Helium has no int() call: every real-to-int conversion in these STEP blocks is a cast written with as int, and the file already imports math at its top, so no STEP block adds an import line.

`main()` does nothing.
